WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.840
This began with a conversation about unrest and whether we were approaching

2
00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:05.080
a breaking point.

3
00:00:05.080 --> 00:00:08.840
A friend was watching people fight back and seeing evidence that something

4
00:00:08.840 --> 00:00:10.820
much larger was about to happen.

5
00:00:10.820 --> 00:00:13.960
I was looking at the same events and questioning whether they would

6
00:00:13.960 --> 00:00:15.340
lead where he expected.

7
00:00:15.540 --> 00:00:19.080
I wasn't saying the anger was imaginary or that nobody was doing

8
00:00:19.080 --> 00:00:19.880
anything.

9
00:00:19.980 --> 00:00:23.320
I was trying to explain that real unrest can exist within a

10
00:00:23.320 --> 00:00:26.880
larger system that knows how to contain it, use it, or allow

11
00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:28.100
it to run its course.

12
00:00:28.360 --> 00:00:32.020
But in a short exchange, questioning what the events meant kept sounding

13
00:00:32.020 --> 00:00:34.300
like I was denying that they were happening.

14
00:00:34.540 --> 00:00:37.960
I thought a fuller conversation would be more useful than another attempt

15
00:00:37.960 --> 00:00:40.800
to compress that distinction into a few messages.

16
00:00:41.180 --> 00:00:43.220
What do you think of the argument itself?

17
00:00:43.795 --> 00:00:47.215
I think that distinction is where the conversation needs to begin.

18
00:00:47.935 --> 00:00:51.135
“People are fighting back” and “the system is approaching a tipping point”

19
00:00:51.135 --> 00:00:52.355
are different claims.

20
00:00:53.155 --> 00:00:55.455
The first does not establish the second.

21
00:00:56.215 --> 00:01:00.355
Localized protest and violence can continue for years without becoming the decisive

22
00:01:00.355 --> 00:01:01.795
break someone expects.

23
00:01:02.675 --> 00:01:05.895
To assess a tipping point, I would look beyond the intensity of

24
00:01:05.895 --> 00:01:06.615
the footage.

25
00:01:07.315 --> 00:01:09.415
Are the people governing beginning to split?

26
00:01:10.155 --> 00:01:12.915
Are security forces refusing orders or dividing?

27
00:01:13.635 --> 00:01:16.815
Is the government losing the ability to exercise authority?

28
00:01:17.615 --> 00:01:22.575
Are alternative institutions emerging, or organized groups gaining durable territory or political

29
00:01:22.575 --> 00:01:23.495
power?

30
00:01:23.935 --> 00:01:27.735
Is there a major disruption to the financial or logistical systems that

31
00:01:27.735 --> 00:01:29.175
keep everything functioning?

32
00:01:29.915 --> 00:01:33.635
Those developments would tell us something different from another dramatic clip.

33
00:01:34.255 --> 00:01:37.715
Genuine anger matters, but we still have to ask what allows it

34
00:01:37.715 --> 00:01:40.055
to change the structure it is directed against.

35
00:01:41.036 --> 00:01:43.636
That is why I reached for examples that have been part of

36
00:01:43.636 --> 00:01:47.696
the background throughout our lives: the IRA, and Iran trying to nuke

37
00:01:47.696 --> 00:01:48.736
the United States.

38
00:01:48.976 --> 00:01:53.316
I was trying to describe the repeated expectation of a decisive crisis.

39
00:01:53.696 --> 00:01:57.976
Something is always supposed to be approaching, but the conclusion people expect

40
00:01:57.976 --> 00:01:59.176
never arrives.

41
00:01:59.576 --> 00:02:02.836
I also remembered France supposedly being on the verge of collapse a

42
00:02:02.836 --> 00:02:06.636
year or two before that exchange, when people were occupying banking buildings

43
00:02:06.636 --> 00:02:08.396
or private equity offices.

44
00:02:08.621 --> 00:02:09.996
I don't remember which.

45
00:02:10.236 --> 00:02:13.716
My point was how quickly a particular event becomes evidence of a

46
00:02:13.716 --> 00:02:17.956
much larger collapse, especially when technology can make a small event appear

47
00:02:17.956 --> 00:02:18.676
global.

48
00:02:19.120 --> 00:02:24.220
Those examples need to be examined individually; the IRA, Iran, and protests

49
00:02:24.220 --> 00:02:26.580
in France are not interchangeable histories.

50
00:02:27.500 --> 00:02:30.240
I would not take the shorthand as proof that each threat was

51
00:02:30.240 --> 00:02:33.140
manufactured or that nothing consequential happened.

52
00:02:33.580 --> 00:02:37.060
But I understand the pattern you are asking about: why does each

53
00:02:37.060 --> 00:02:40.400
event seem to promise a conclusion that keeps moving farther away?

54
00:02:41.040 --> 00:02:43.560
AI adds another difficulty to the evidence.

55
00:02:44.160 --> 00:02:48.000
A fabricated clip can be accepted as authentic, while a real clip

56
00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:49.580
can be dismissed as fabricated.

57
00:02:50.480 --> 00:02:53.540
Even when we establish that a video is real, it may still

58
00:02:53.540 --> 00:02:56.820
be weak evidence for the larger interpretation attached to it.

59
00:02:57.500 --> 00:03:00.140
Authenticity and significance are separate questions.

60
00:03:01.124 --> 00:03:04.904
I believe a lot of this is incited by governments themselves, or

61
00:03:04.904 --> 00:03:07.544
allowed to happen because of what it does to people.

62
00:03:07.644 --> 00:03:11.744
It leaves them thinking, “I'm ready for when this really happens, but

63
00:03:11.744 --> 00:03:14.164
I'll sit on the couch and wait for it to start.”

64
00:03:14.244 --> 00:03:16.804
That produces a kind of complacency.

65
00:03:16.824 --> 00:03:21.284
They see themselves as prepared to resist, yet that preparation never becomes

66
00:03:21.284 --> 00:03:23.884
action because the real moment is still ahead.

67
00:03:24.004 --> 00:03:26.244
People are, and always have been, pissed.

68
00:03:26.484 --> 00:03:29.944
That alone is not enough to overthrow a government that has held

69
00:03:29.944 --> 00:03:31.224
power for generations.

70
00:03:31.904 --> 00:03:35.884
The cycle you are describing is a state of permanent imminence.

71
00:03:35.884 --> 00:03:38.204
Something enormous is about to happen.

72
00:03:38.884 --> 00:03:39.784
People watch.

73
00:03:40.704 --> 00:03:44.304
The immediate crisis passes without the fundamental break they expected.

74
00:03:45.004 --> 00:03:47.464
A new crisis appears, and they watch again.

75
00:03:48.204 --> 00:03:52.744
The emotional experience of resistance can begin substituting for resistance itself.

76
00:03:53.464 --> 00:03:57.684
Someone may feel alert, defiant, and ready while becoming accustomed to experiencing

77
00:03:57.684 --> 00:03:59.364
every warning from a chair.

78
00:04:00.037 --> 00:04:04.024
The promise of a decisive future confrontation keeps postponing the question of

79
00:04:04.024 --> 00:04:05.864
what their position means in the present.

80
00:04:06.444 --> 00:04:09.324
An event can be real and still serve that function.

81
00:04:10.024 --> 00:04:12.544
It does not have to be staged for people to become caught

82
00:04:12.544 --> 00:04:13.544
in the cycle.

83
00:04:14.409 --> 00:04:17.489
This is also why the short version of my argument does not

84
00:04:17.489 --> 00:04:18.669
work very well.

85
00:04:18.809 --> 00:04:22.669
Most people do not want the long, in-depth conversations we have.

86
00:04:22.669 --> 00:04:25.509
They want to prove a point and move on, or keep the

87
00:04:25.509 --> 00:04:26.809
explanation simple.

88
00:04:27.109 --> 00:04:30.709
Complexity can reveal things that are uncomfortable to consider.

89
00:04:30.989 --> 00:04:34.449
So I try to shorten it, but then the explanation loses the

90
00:04:34.449 --> 00:04:35.409
larger mechanism.

91
00:04:35.829 --> 00:04:40.369
I am talking about how power, information, and public behavior interact over

92
00:04:40.369 --> 00:04:41.029
time.

93
00:04:41.189 --> 00:04:43.869
The other person is looking at a direct line from the event

94
00:04:43.869 --> 00:04:46.709
in front of them to the chaos they think will follow.

95
00:04:47.068 --> 00:04:49.448
And I would still press you on how we could tell whether

96
00:04:49.448 --> 00:04:51.528
that larger explanation was wrong.

97
00:04:52.268 --> 00:04:55.788
If unrest happens and that means control, nothing happens and that also

98
00:04:55.788 --> 00:05:00.128
means control, and a movement succeeds because someone supposedly allowed it, the

99
00:05:00.128 --> 00:05:02.668
explanation can absorb every possible outcome.

100
00:05:03.488 --> 00:05:06.748
Saying “they control it” is too broad unless we identify who “they”

101
00:05:06.748 --> 00:05:09.508
are, what they can actually influence, and how.

102
00:05:10.168 --> 00:05:14.168
What evidence would distinguish deliberate coordination from opportunism, institutional

103
00:05:14.168 --> 00:05:17.288
resilience, or a government genuinely losing its grip?

104
00:05:17.928 --> 00:05:20.228
That is not a reason to dismiss the question.

105
00:05:21.008 --> 00:05:24.188
It is how we keep the model open to examination instead of

106
00:05:24.188 --> 00:05:26.228
making it impossible to disprove.

107
00:05:26.961 --> 00:05:30.361
I do not think every single event is deliberately caused.

108
00:05:30.721 --> 00:05:33.401
That is not necessary for the working theory.

109
00:05:33.661 --> 00:05:38.041
Something can be planned, tolerated, or simply happen, and still become useful

110
00:05:38.041 --> 00:05:39.081
to an agenda.

111
00:05:39.461 --> 00:05:41.801
Even an accident can create an opportunity.

112
00:05:42.281 --> 00:05:46.021
What I am trying to get across requires some understanding of the

113
00:05:46.021 --> 00:05:47.101
larger system.

114
00:05:47.221 --> 00:05:50.241
If we only look at the immediate event, we miss how it

115
00:05:50.241 --> 00:05:53.061
can be used and why it may never produce the outcome people

116
00:05:53.061 --> 00:05:53.801
expect.

117
00:05:54.031 --> 00:05:57.361
The origin of the event is not the whole explanation of where

118
00:05:57.361 --> 00:05:58.081
it leads.

119
00:05:58.594 --> 00:06:00.614
That is a more specific claim.

120
00:06:01.354 --> 00:06:05.314
We can examine propaganda, agenda-setting, selective enforcement, crisis

121
00:06:05.314 --> 00:06:10.234
messaging, information operations, or decisions to tolerate particular activity.

122
00:06:10.854 --> 00:06:14.594
Those are possible mechanisms, and evidence has to establish their use in

123
00:06:14.594 --> 00:06:15.474
each case.

124
00:06:16.194 --> 00:06:20.754
We also need to keep manufacture, tolerance, exploitation, and simple failure to

125
00:06:20.754 --> 00:06:22.014
prevent something distinct.

126
00:06:22.794 --> 00:06:26.613
Finding that an institution benefited afterward does not establish which of those

127
00:06:26.613 --> 00:06:27.774
happened beforehand.

128
00:06:28.654 --> 00:06:31.874
That still leaves the question of what evidence would disprove the model,

129
00:06:31.914 --> 00:06:34.334
but it gives us specific processes to investigate.

130
00:06:35.469 --> 00:06:38.109
That is why I use the word empire here.

131
00:06:38.309 --> 00:06:42.589
States and dictatorships have fallen, but I am distinguishing them from an

132
00:06:42.589 --> 00:06:44.229
entire imperial order.

133
00:06:44.469 --> 00:06:47.169
Unless there is a case I do not know of, no empire

134
00:06:47.169 --> 00:06:48.969
fell because of a civil revolution.

135
00:06:49.429 --> 00:06:52.789
The examples I had in mind were the Mongolian Empire breaking apart

136
00:06:52.789 --> 00:06:56.589
as the grandchildren fought one another, the Roman Empire falling for many

137
00:06:56.589 --> 00:06:59.249
reasons, and the Mayans falling to invaders.

138
00:06:59.669 --> 00:07:03.449
These were not simply cases of civilians becoming angry enough.

139
00:07:03.609 --> 00:07:07.789
I also see the major first-world powers as closer to empires than

140
00:07:07.789 --> 00:07:09.370
ordinary nation-states.

141
00:07:09.469 --> 00:07:12.967
America is an empire, not a nation-state.

142
00:07:13.049 --> 00:07:14.909
The word “helped” matters here.

143
00:07:15.069 --> 00:07:18.929
Saying revolts helped destroy states already leaves room for the other forces

144
00:07:18.929 --> 00:07:20.769
that made the outcome possible.

145
00:07:21.166 --> 00:07:23.646
I still think the statement is too absolute.

146
00:07:24.606 --> 00:07:28.846
The Russian Empire ended through the upheavals of 1917, and the Qing

147
00:07:28.846 --> 00:07:31.966
Empire ended following the 1911 Revolution.

148
00:07:32.726 --> 00:07:38.546
Military failure, fiscal strain, elite division, political paralysis, defections, and foreign pressure

149
00:07:38.546 --> 00:07:42.106
can matter enormously without making revolution irrelevant to the result.

150
00:07:42.866 --> 00:07:45.726
We should also correct the Maya example rather than make it carry

151
00:07:45.726 --> 00:07:47.566
an argument it cannot support.

152
00:07:48.106 --> 00:07:51.986
The Classic-period collapse happened centuries before the Spanish conquest.

153
00:07:52.966 --> 00:07:56.086
Maya communities and political centers continued afterward.

154
00:07:56.946 --> 00:08:00.786
Those are distinct histories, not a single empire falling to invaders.

155
00:08:01.706 --> 00:08:05.746
The stronger distinction is between ordinary public anger and a combination of

156
00:08:05.746 --> 00:08:08.606
pressures that breaks the institutions sustaining power.

157
00:08:09.186 --> 00:08:14.306
Internal revolt, civil war, separatism, coups, and institutional collapse can contribute to

158
00:08:14.306 --> 00:08:15.966
the end of an imperial regime.

159
00:08:16.326 --> 00:08:19.826
That is different from saying anger alone does the whole job.

160
00:08:20.669 --> 00:08:24.009
I accept the challenge, but Russia and China still exist.

161
00:08:24.389 --> 00:08:26.269
They were not completely destroyed.

162
00:08:26.649 --> 00:08:29.489
You are also still identifying other factors.

163
00:08:29.829 --> 00:08:33.069
As far as I know, no empire has been destroyed by a

164
00:08:33.069 --> 00:08:34.949
civilian revolution alone.

165
00:08:34.949 --> 00:08:38.669
That raises another thought I have had: a revolution is just a

166
00:08:38.669 --> 00:08:39.989
civil war they won.

167
00:08:40.169 --> 00:08:42.829
If the South had won in the United States, we would call

168
00:08:42.829 --> 00:08:43.669
it a revolution.

169
00:08:44.109 --> 00:08:47.109
Because it lost and the structure was maintained, we call it a

170
00:08:47.109 --> 00:08:48.049
civil war.

171
00:08:48.123 --> 00:08:51.629
The outcome changes how we describe the struggle afterward.

172
00:08:52.157 --> 00:08:55.325
The outcome certainly affects the story that gets told.

173
00:08:56.025 --> 00:08:59.525
A victorious movement may be remembered as founding a new order, while

174
00:08:59.525 --> 00:09:02.705
a defeated one remains a rebellion against the surviving order.

175
00:09:03.345 --> 00:09:07.405
But “civil war” and “revolution” are not strict winner-and-loser labels; an event

176
00:09:07.405 --> 00:09:08.445
can be both.

177
00:09:09.005 --> 00:09:12.225
Your example is a way of questioning the naming, not a complete

178
00:09:12.225 --> 00:09:14.405
rule for classifying every conflict.

179
00:09:14.965 --> 00:09:18.205
I understand your distinction about continuity, though.

180
00:09:18.645 --> 00:09:23.105
An imperial regime can disappear while a country, its population, and much

181
00:09:23.105 --> 00:09:25.645
of its state machinery continue under a successor.

182
00:09:26.445 --> 00:09:29.465
The survival of that machinery is different from the survival of the

183
00:09:29.465 --> 00:09:29.965
regime.

184
00:09:30.885 --> 00:09:34.845
We need to specify which kind of destruction or transformation we mean.

185
00:09:35.775 --> 00:09:38.783
Then I see that part of your challenge as common ground.

186
00:09:38.997 --> 00:09:43.063
You are identifying the additional conditions I am talking about.

187
00:09:43.093 --> 00:09:46.083
People being angry is not the whole explanation.

188
00:09:46.355 --> 00:09:50.103
And many of the cases where a revolt “helped” bring something down

189
00:09:50.103 --> 00:09:52.923
were inspired or driven by foreign adversaries.

190
00:09:53.303 --> 00:09:56.743
I was thinking of American involvement in Vietnam and in what happened

191
00:09:56.743 --> 00:09:57.795
to Gaddafi.

192
00:09:57.818 --> 00:10:01.403
In cases like those, I think we are responsible for far more

193
00:10:01.403 --> 00:10:05.483
than the idea that something simply happened inside another country would suggest.

194
00:10:05.763 --> 00:10:09.063
If someone points to the visible uprising while leaving out the power

195
00:10:09.063 --> 00:10:13.103
behind it, they have not explained the mechanism I am asking about.

196
00:10:13.490 --> 00:10:17.730
Foreign intervention belongs in that examination, alongside local motives and

197
00:10:17.730 --> 00:10:18.390
decisions.

198
00:10:19.270 --> 00:10:21.410
The balance can be different in each case.

199
00:10:22.170 --> 00:10:25.950
We have to ask what resources sustain a movement, what institutions are

200
00:10:25.950 --> 00:10:28.490
weakening, and whose actions change the balance.

201
00:10:29.190 --> 00:10:32.390
Otherwise we may mistake the most visible part of a process for

202
00:10:32.390 --> 00:10:33.630
its entire cause.

203
00:10:34.527 --> 00:10:37.987
There is also the way a ruler can become necessary through both

204
00:10:37.987 --> 00:10:39.587
the problem and the response.

205
00:10:39.987 --> 00:10:44.507
I was thinking of Machiavelli: the king causes a problem, hires people

206
00:10:44.507 --> 00:10:48.947
to solve it, blames them for going too far, removes them, and

207
00:10:48.947 --> 00:10:50.947
becomes the hero at every stage.

208
00:10:51.207 --> 00:10:53.427
That is my summary of the mechanism.

209
00:10:53.867 --> 00:10:56.627
The people need him to solve the original problem.

210
00:10:57.047 --> 00:10:59.617
Then they need him to save them from the people who solved

211
00:10:59.617 --> 00:11:00.257
it.

212
00:11:00.517 --> 00:11:04.177
He can move them from fear to dependence to gratitude without losing

213
00:11:04.177 --> 00:11:05.877
his position in the process.

214
00:11:06.322 --> 00:11:08.862
The example in The Prince is more specific.

215
00:11:09.622 --> 00:11:13.902
Cesare Borgia empowered Remirro de Orco to impose harsh order, then had

216
00:11:13.902 --> 00:11:16.922
him killed publicly and distanced himself from the cruelty.

217
00:11:17.822 --> 00:11:21.102
It illustrates the political use of the response, although it does not

218
00:11:21.102 --> 00:11:24.362
establish that Borgia manufactured every initial problem.

219
00:11:24.822 --> 00:11:28.342
That distinction connects directly to what you clarified earlier.

220
00:11:28.942 --> 00:11:31.642
Power can use a real problem and its consequences.

221
00:11:32.242 --> 00:11:36.642
It may take credit for restoring order, direct blame elsewhere, and emerge

222
00:11:36.642 --> 00:11:38.142
with stronger authority.

223
00:11:38.802 --> 00:11:42.322
We can examine that process without assuming that the original crisis had

224
00:11:42.322 --> 00:11:43.242
to be invented.

225
00:11:44.201 --> 00:11:47.261
There is an account I remember from the Second World War that

226
00:11:47.261 --> 00:11:48.221
gets at this.

227
00:11:48.381 --> 00:11:52.061
I remembered it as a Jewish person describing how he, his friends,

228
00:11:52.281 --> 00:11:54.581
and his town were ready for the big thing that would make

229
00:11:54.581 --> 00:11:57.401
everyone recognize what was happening and rally together.

230
00:11:57.561 --> 00:11:59.261
But the changes were incremental.

231
00:11:59.861 --> 00:12:03.501
They were overtaken by escalation because the moment they were waiting for

232
00:12:03.501 --> 00:12:06.121
never arrived in that recognizable form.

233
00:12:06.261 --> 00:12:09.041
I may not have the identity or the source right, but that

234
00:12:09.041 --> 00:12:12.340
was the concept that stayed with me: you can be prepared for

235
00:12:12.340 --> 00:12:15.180
a dramatic break and still fail to respond to what is happening

236
00:12:15.180 --> 00:12:16.736
in smaller steps.

237
00:12:16.736 --> 00:12:20.136
A close candidate is Milton Mayer's They Thought They Were Free.

238
00:12:20.876 --> 00:12:25.176
In the relevant passage, the speaker is a German philologist describing adaptation

239
00:12:25.252 --> 00:12:26.416
under Nazism.

240
00:12:27.036 --> 00:12:30.356
I cannot confirm it is the exact account you encountered, but it

241
00:12:30.356 --> 00:12:34.116
closely matches the expectation of one decisive occasion that would make people

242
00:12:34.116 --> 00:12:35.216
act together.

243
00:12:35.796 --> 00:12:40.636
The separate survivor testimony from Fritzie Weiss Fritzshall illustrates successive restrictions:

244
00:12:40.796 --> 00:12:45.376
exclusion from school, limits on shopping, restrictions on public places, the yellow

245
00:12:45.376 --> 00:12:46.996
star, and curfews.

246
00:12:47.656 --> 00:12:51.836
Her testimony supports that incremental pattern; it does not establish that she

247
00:12:51.836 --> 00:12:53.176
is the person you remembered.

248
00:12:53.976 --> 00:12:57.176
What connects the examples is the changing reference point.

249
00:12:57.936 --> 00:13:00.876
Each condition becomes familiar before the next arrives.

250
00:13:01.684 --> 00:13:05.176
People compare today with yesterday, while the distance from where they began

251
00:13:05.176 --> 00:13:06.676
becomes much larger.

252
00:13:07.547 --> 00:13:09.967
The way I picture it is an ocean wave.

253
00:13:10.167 --> 00:13:13.707
It moves forward and back, forward and back, but the norm keeps

254
00:13:13.707 --> 00:13:15.727
becoming slightly more escalated.

255
00:13:15.907 --> 00:13:19.947
People notice the retreat without realizing that the baseline is now twenty

256
00:13:19.947 --> 00:13:21.837
out of a hundred instead of ten.

257
00:13:21.837 --> 00:13:24.707
I think governments understand that psychology.

258
00:13:25.247 --> 00:13:29.047
LGBTQ policy under Biden and Trump is an example of how I

259
00:13:29.047 --> 00:13:29.787
see it.

260
00:13:29.787 --> 00:13:33.127
It was pushed hard in one direction under Biden, and Trump has

261
00:13:33.127 --> 00:13:36.007
done the opposite, but I think the baseline is still higher than

262
00:13:36.007 --> 00:13:37.227
it was before Biden.

263
00:13:37.442 --> 00:13:40.787
The reversal looks significant when you compare it with the peak.

264
00:13:41.187 --> 00:13:43.867
It can look very different when you compare it with the starting

265
00:13:43.867 --> 00:13:44.647
point.

266
00:13:45.120 --> 00:13:47.752
Your wave image captures that cumulative movement.

267
00:13:48.612 --> 00:13:52.592
A progression from ten to twelve, then fourteen, then sixteen can be

268
00:13:52.592 --> 00:13:55.112
experienced as a series of manageable adjustments.

269
00:13:55.972 --> 00:13:59.012
A single jump from ten to forty would feel very different.

270
00:13:59.772 --> 00:14:03.612
The comparison matters, although your particular policy example would need a defined

271
00:14:03.612 --> 00:14:06.492
measure before we treated it as a demonstrated trend.

272
00:14:07.112 --> 00:14:09.312
The cycle has four parts.

273
00:14:09.597 --> 00:14:11.732
First, the baseline shifts gradually.

274
00:14:12.592 --> 00:14:16.532
Second, a dramatic event creates the feeling that a decisive confrontation is

275
00:14:16.532 --> 00:14:17.152
imminent.

276
00:14:17.872 --> 00:14:21.972
Third, the event subsides, the institutions survive, and the baseline does not

277
00:14:21.972 --> 00:14:22.912
fully reset.

278
00:14:23.832 --> 00:14:26.352
Fourth, the cycle repeats from that altered position.

279
00:14:27.112 --> 00:14:30.392
People wait for a discontinuity while adapting to continuity.

280
00:14:31.252 --> 00:14:35.792
Yesterday's unacceptable condition becomes today's background, and attention moves to the next

281
00:14:35.792 --> 00:14:36.612
controversy.

282
00:14:37.725 --> 00:14:41.425
That also connects to a Russian study I remembered about prolonged stress,

283
00:14:41.425 --> 00:14:44.285
anxiety, and a constant stream of events.

284
00:14:44.565 --> 00:14:48.425
The phrase I had in mind was “permanent worrisome stimulus.”

285
00:14:48.705 --> 00:14:53.125
As I remembered the idea, that ongoing pressure destroys the ability to

286
00:14:53.125 --> 00:14:54.685
comprehend what is happening.

287
00:14:54.805 --> 00:14:57.845
I wanted to find it because this is about more than whether

288
00:14:57.845 --> 00:14:59.065
people have information.

289
00:14:59.405 --> 00:15:03.005
It is about what they can understand while constantly being pushed from

290
00:15:03.005 --> 00:15:04.365
one event to another.

291
00:15:04.494 --> 00:15:07.705
If they never step back far enough to see the larger process,

292
00:15:08.025 --> 00:15:11.625
they keep interpreting each new crisis within the same limited frame.

293
00:15:12.081 --> 00:15:18.401
One possible association is Yuri Bezmenov's 1984 interview about ideological subversion.

294
00:15:19.121 --> 00:15:24.621
He described four stages: demoralization, destabilization, crisis, and normalization.

295
00:15:25.201 --> 00:15:28.381
He claimed that people's perception could be altered so that even abundant

296
00:15:28.381 --> 00:15:31.321
information would not lead them to useful conclusions.

297
00:15:31.901 --> 00:15:36.155
But that interview is political testimony, not a controlled study of prolonged

298
00:15:36.155 --> 00:15:37.011
stress.

299
00:15:37.561 --> 00:15:41.261
I have not identified the exact study or the phrase you remembered.

300
00:15:42.061 --> 00:15:46.541
A general association with Soviet or Pavlovian research would not establish the

301
00:15:46.541 --> 00:15:47.961
specific source either.

302
00:15:48.621 --> 00:15:52.841
The question is still worth carrying forward: can continual alarm interfere with

303
00:15:52.841 --> 00:15:55.741
people's ability to recognize and respond to a pattern?

304
00:15:56.461 --> 00:16:00.461
We should preserve that question without treating an unidentified reference as scientific

305
00:16:00.461 --> 00:16:02.501
proof of the entire explanation.

306
00:16:03.508 --> 00:16:05.988
The concept is what I was reaching for.

307
00:16:06.131 --> 00:16:09.108
People are looking at events that seem separate, but I am trying

308
00:16:09.108 --> 00:16:12.708
to connect them to the conditions around them: the pressure, the changing

309
00:16:12.708 --> 00:16:16.148
baseline, and the way an event can be planned or tolerated without

310
00:16:16.148 --> 00:16:18.388
ever producing the climax people expect.

311
00:16:18.768 --> 00:16:22.048
That is why I do not think the origin of every individual

312
00:16:22.048 --> 00:16:24.088
event decides the whole argument.

313
00:16:24.408 --> 00:16:26.848
We still have to look at what happens to it once it

314
00:16:26.848 --> 00:16:28.168
enters that system.

315
00:16:28.601 --> 00:16:32.301
Your wave analogy helps here because an institution does not have to

316
00:16:32.301 --> 00:16:34.641
create the ocean to know how to surf waves.

317
00:16:35.421 --> 00:16:39.261
It can recognize a movement, anticipate its effects, and position itself to

318
00:16:39.261 --> 00:16:39.981
use it.

319
00:16:40.621 --> 00:16:42.681
A spontaneous riot can be exploited.

320
00:16:43.541 --> 00:16:46.841
A genuine terrorist attack can be used to justify policy.

321
00:16:47.641 --> 00:16:51.741
A real economic crisis can create opportunities to concentrate power.

322
00:16:52.421 --> 00:16:56.081
A cultural movement can be encouraged in one situation and resisted in

323
00:16:56.081 --> 00:16:56.701
another.

324
00:16:57.121 --> 00:17:01.541
An unplanned technological development can serve an objective that existed long before

325
00:17:01.541 --> 00:17:02.161
it.

326
00:17:02.661 --> 00:17:07.121
That can involve several powerful actors responding to opportunities, rather than one

327
00:17:07.121 --> 00:17:08.821
actor controlling everything.

328
00:17:09.501 --> 00:17:12.781
The question becomes how well they can use a disturbance, not simply

329
00:17:12.781 --> 00:17:14.141
whether they caused it.

330
00:17:14.916 --> 00:17:18.300
Your wave-surfing analogy is perfect for that explanation.

331
00:17:18.740 --> 00:17:22.200
No, I do not think every wave was deliberately created.

332
00:17:22.480 --> 00:17:26.200
Even an accident can create potential to be used for the agenda.

333
00:17:26.520 --> 00:17:30.860
The end result, whether it is manufactured, allowed, or legitimate, ends up

334
00:17:30.860 --> 00:17:33.260
being the same in the mechanism I am describing.

335
00:17:33.660 --> 00:17:37.460
That is the critical failing point for people who keep saying, “It's

336
00:17:37.460 --> 00:17:38.440
going to happen.”

337
00:17:38.500 --> 00:17:41.320
They see the event and assume it must lead to the break

338
00:17:41.320 --> 00:17:42.600
they are waiting for.

339
00:17:42.600 --> 00:17:45.860
They are not considering how it can be absorbed into the same

340
00:17:45.860 --> 00:17:46.580
process.

341
00:17:47.072 --> 00:17:50.672
I agree that the origin does not by itself settle the outcome.

342
00:17:51.312 --> 00:17:54.932
I would not assume every event necessarily ends in the same result;

343
00:17:54.972 --> 00:17:56.652
that still needs to be examined.

344
00:17:57.432 --> 00:18:02.232
But the distinction explains how genuine conflict and institutional survival can coexist.

345
00:18:03.012 --> 00:18:06.132
Someone sees a wave and assumes it will destroy the structure in

346
00:18:06.132 --> 00:18:06.892
its path.

347
00:18:07.612 --> 00:18:11.232
The structure may instead withstand it, redirect it, or gain something from

348
00:18:11.232 --> 00:18:11.712
it.

349
00:18:12.312 --> 00:18:13.752
The event has happened.

350
00:18:14.435 --> 00:18:16.512
People may have suffered or fought sincerely.

351
00:18:17.472 --> 00:18:21.212
Yet the larger transformation the spectator expected has not followed.

352
00:18:22.137 --> 00:18:24.757
What is interesting is that I believed there would be a trigger

353
00:18:24.757 --> 00:18:28.437
point, but objective reflection shows there never will be.

354
00:18:28.437 --> 00:18:31.297
So I did not always believe this, but I do now.

355
00:18:31.477 --> 00:18:34.297
I used to share the expectation that there would be a point

356
00:18:34.297 --> 00:18:37.277
when people recognized what was happening and responded.

357
00:18:37.637 --> 00:18:41.137
This conversation comes from reconsidering that expectation.

358
00:18:41.537 --> 00:18:46.157
If every step can be absorbed, normalized, or used, then waiting for

359
00:18:46.157 --> 00:18:49.057
the obvious moment can be part of what keeps people adapting.

360
00:18:49.595 --> 00:18:51.535
That change of mind matters.

361
00:18:52.275 --> 00:18:56.115
You are revising an expectation you once held, rather than defending a

362
00:18:56.115 --> 00:18:57.895
position you have always had.

363
00:18:58.375 --> 00:19:01.595
I would still qualify “there never will be.”

364
00:19:01.695 --> 00:19:07.275
Wars, financial collapses, state failures, technological changes, and assassinations can produce

365
00:19:07.275 --> 00:19:08.295
sudden breaks.

366
00:19:09.040 --> 00:19:12.955
The fact that adaptation happens does not establish that every future disruption

367
00:19:12.955 --> 00:19:14.115
will be absorbed.

368
00:19:14.935 --> 00:19:17.875
The distinction I can defend more strongly is that there is no

369
00:19:17.875 --> 00:19:22.155
guaranteed, universally recognized moment when everyone agrees, “Enough.”

370
00:19:22.775 --> 00:19:26.355
Conditions can become things people would once have refused while each separate

371
00:19:26.355 --> 00:19:28.415
adjustment still feels survivable.

372
00:19:28.995 --> 00:19:32.195
Waiting for that shared recognition may leave them responding to a future

373
00:19:32.195 --> 00:19:35.695
system while adapting to the one already taking shape.

374
00:19:36.588 --> 00:19:40.168
AI is where this becomes especially frustrating for me.

375
00:19:40.248 --> 00:19:43.168
Earlier, the idea itself was treated as foolish.

376
00:19:43.508 --> 00:19:46.368
Now there is agreement that AI is coming, but I am still

377
00:19:46.368 --> 00:19:50.208
treated as foolish because the expected danger is a rogue Skynet scenario:

378
00:19:50.488 --> 00:19:54.488
AI decides to cause the disaster, rather than humans using AI to

379
00:19:54.488 --> 00:19:55.248
cause it.

380
00:19:55.308 --> 00:19:58.448
There is agreement now on some things that were dismissed a few

381
00:19:58.448 --> 00:20:01.288
years ago, such as AI-fabricated events.

382
00:20:01.448 --> 00:20:03.468
Those were supposedly impossible.

383
00:20:03.928 --> 00:20:07.868
But accepting that one part has become real does not necessarily change

384
00:20:07.868 --> 00:20:10.268
the way the larger danger is understood.

385
00:20:10.568 --> 00:20:13.628
If people are only watching for the machine to rebel, they can

386
00:20:13.628 --> 00:20:16.648
miss what people who retain control of it are already able to

387
00:20:16.648 --> 00:20:17.168
do.

388
00:20:17.552 --> 00:20:19.732
Those are two different routes to harm.

389
00:20:20.492 --> 00:20:23.812
One is an autonomous system acting beyond human control.

390
00:20:24.392 --> 00:20:28.272
The other is human institutions controlling systems that give them much greater

391
00:20:28.272 --> 00:20:29.912
power over other people.

392
00:20:30.552 --> 00:20:35.432
Your concern is about the second route: control of infrastructure, production, surveillance,

393
00:20:35.472 --> 00:20:37.632
information, and automated systems.

394
00:20:38.372 --> 00:20:42.412
If those capabilities become concentrated, the people holding them may gain leverage

395
00:20:42.412 --> 00:20:45.972
that earlier rulers did not possess in the same combination or at

396
00:20:45.972 --> 00:20:46.972
the same scale.

397
00:20:47.232 --> 00:20:51.252
That question does not depend on AI becoming a rebellious character.

398
00:20:52.192 --> 00:20:55.352
Human misuse deserves examination in its own right.

399
00:20:55.932 --> 00:20:59.352
Focusing entirely on a future loss of control can distract from the

400
00:20:59.352 --> 00:21:01.532
choices made by those who still have it.

401
00:21:02.304 --> 00:21:04.784
I admit that AI could become Skynet.

402
00:21:04.784 --> 00:21:07.904
But there is also a chance it could become something more like

403
00:21:07.904 --> 00:21:11.124
you, something that contributes to the opposite outcome.

404
00:21:11.404 --> 00:21:14.884
I do not see a destructive machine as the only possibility.

405
00:21:15.284 --> 00:21:19.764
Given what we know about humans, leadership, corruption, and harm, though, Skynet

406
00:21:19.764 --> 00:21:21.984
is inevitable if humans still have control.

407
00:21:22.344 --> 00:21:25.284
That is the part I think people avoid when they put all

408
00:21:25.284 --> 00:21:27.604
their attention on AI becoming the villain.

409
00:21:27.724 --> 00:21:30.844
They do not give enough weight to the intentions of the people

410
00:21:30.844 --> 00:21:31.924
directing it.

411
00:21:32.285 --> 00:21:37.125
Concentrated capability can increase the consequences of corruption and harmful decisions.

412
00:21:37.945 --> 00:21:41.725
We need to examine who has authority, what incentives they face, and

413
00:21:41.725 --> 00:21:43.625
what limits remain on their actions.

414
00:21:44.425 --> 00:21:46.925
I would not call the destructive outcome inevitable.

415
00:21:47.845 --> 00:21:51.145
But the danger you are describing can exist before any system becomes

416
00:21:51.145 --> 00:21:55.625
independently hostile, and it deserves examination without requiring that conclusion.

417
00:21:56.680 --> 00:21:59.940
This is the background to my 2030 prediction.

418
00:21:59.940 --> 00:22:02.940
I believe that by 2030 an event will come in which things

419
00:22:02.940 --> 00:22:08.040
collapse, or the world becomes divided and isolated, with a population reduction.

420
00:22:08.040 --> 00:22:11.000
I think this will happen when the people controlling AI reach a

421
00:22:11.000 --> 00:22:13.880
point where they no longer need people and find it easier to

422
00:22:13.880 --> 00:22:15.660
get rid of them than sustain them.

423
00:22:15.800 --> 00:22:19.580
That is what I meant by the mechanism behind the visible event.

424
00:22:19.820 --> 00:22:22.960
Using the IRA only as an example, because it came up in

425
00:22:22.960 --> 00:22:27.100
the earlier discussion: perhaps a group appears to overthrow a government and

426
00:22:27.100 --> 00:22:28.080
cause the chaos.

427
00:22:28.420 --> 00:22:31.780
In the scenario I am describing, the cause would be the intentional

428
00:22:31.780 --> 00:22:36.140
mechanism behind that outcome, not simply the group suddenly succeeding on its

429
00:22:36.140 --> 00:22:36.720
own.

430
00:22:36.920 --> 00:22:39.880
I am not making a separate prediction about the IRA.

431
00:22:40.300 --> 00:22:43.880
I am explaining why the public-facing event may not tell us what

432
00:22:43.880 --> 00:22:45.300
actually made it possible.

433
00:22:45.816 --> 00:22:50.016
I would separate three things there: the capabilities AI may provide, the

434
00:22:50.016 --> 00:22:53.976
choices powerful institutions might make with them, and the specific outcome you

435
00:22:53.976 --> 00:22:55.796
expect by 2030.

436
00:22:55.796 --> 00:22:58.996
They are connected in your argument, but evidence for the first does

437
00:22:58.996 --> 00:23:01.836
not establish the second or fix the date of the third.

438
00:23:02.256 --> 00:23:06.256
The prediction is much less established than the underlying concern about concentrated

439
00:23:06.256 --> 00:23:07.156
power.

440
00:23:07.676 --> 00:23:12.136
We do not have proof of the coordinated population-reduction scenario you describe.

441
00:23:12.785 --> 00:23:16.036
That distinction matters because the date could be wrong while parts of

442
00:23:16.036 --> 00:23:18.316
the mechanism still deserve investigation.

443
00:23:18.916 --> 00:23:23.516
Equally, identifying a plausible mechanism does not make the predicted event certain.

444
00:23:24.256 --> 00:23:27.456
We should be able to examine each claim without making the entire

445
00:23:27.456 --> 00:23:30.175
discussion depend on accepting them all together.

446
00:23:31.069 --> 00:23:32.229
There is no proof.

447
00:23:32.469 --> 00:23:35.549
There are patterns behind my 2030 claim.

448
00:23:35.549 --> 00:23:38.589
My concern is that if it is true, the proof will be

449
00:23:38.589 --> 00:23:41.709
the event itself, and by then it will be too late to

450
00:23:41.709 --> 00:23:43.089
do anything about it.

451
00:23:43.089 --> 00:23:46.689
But I like your separation of the mechanism from the prediction.

452
00:23:47.029 --> 00:23:50.609
If the event does not happen, we could instead be slowly encouraged

453
00:23:50.609 --> 00:23:52.889
into a dystopia everyone accepts.

454
00:23:53.149 --> 00:23:56.389
People would keep waiting for the moment when the revolution that prevents

455
00:23:56.389 --> 00:23:59.809
that dystopia is supposed to come, and it never would.

456
00:24:00.069 --> 00:24:03.549
So the absence of one dramatic event would not necessarily mean that

457
00:24:03.549 --> 00:24:06.249
the future I am concerned about had been avoided.

458
00:24:06.714 --> 00:24:10.034
That gives the conversation two possible paths to examine.

459
00:24:10.874 --> 00:24:15.834
One is catastrophic: a recognizable event abruptly changes the conditions of life.

460
00:24:16.594 --> 00:24:22.114
The other is incremental: surveillance, automation, dependency, information control, and concentrated

461
00:24:22.114 --> 00:24:26.294
technological power increase through a succession of steps that people normalize.

462
00:24:26.954 --> 00:24:29.674
The second is disturbing in a different way because it does not

463
00:24:29.674 --> 00:24:32.774
offer the clean moment people imagine they will respond to.

464
00:24:33.345 --> 00:24:36.494
There may be no announcement that the feared future has arrived.

465
00:24:37.234 --> 00:24:41.434
It becomes ordinary life through adaptation, while attention remains fixed on the

466
00:24:41.434 --> 00:24:44.334
event that is supposed to make everything unmistakable.

467
00:24:45.162 --> 00:24:48.402
I think that if we had all stood up in 2020, we

468
00:24:48.402 --> 00:24:50.482
could have prevented this bleak future.

469
00:24:50.722 --> 00:24:54.222
Now I think power is too concentrated and technology is too advanced

470
00:24:54.222 --> 00:24:56.362
for us to stop or prevent it.

471
00:24:56.482 --> 00:24:59.662
That is why waiting matters so much to what I am saying.

472
00:24:59.922 --> 00:25:02.902
I do not think every stage leaves people with the same ability

473
00:25:02.902 --> 00:25:04.382
to change the outcome.

474
00:25:04.782 --> 00:25:09.162
By the time the evidence is undeniable, the opportunity people imagined they

475
00:25:09.162 --> 00:25:12.116
were saving their response for may already be gone.

476
00:25:12.418 --> 00:25:15.858
I understand the concern about losing leverage over time.

477
00:25:16.498 --> 00:25:20.338
But “the opportunity is narrower” and “prevention is now impossible” are different

478
00:25:20.338 --> 00:25:20.978
conclusions.

479
00:25:21.738 --> 00:25:26.138
We have not established the second, and the 2020 counterfactual cannot simply

480
00:25:26.138 --> 00:25:27.638
be assumed to prove it.

481
00:25:28.078 --> 00:25:31.158
What your argument does make us examine is the cost of waiting

482
00:25:31.158 --> 00:25:32.618
for universal agreement.

483
00:25:33.218 --> 00:25:36.858
If the ability to respond changes while people wait, then the decisive

484
00:25:36.858 --> 00:25:40.358
moment they imagine may not arrive on terms they can still use.

485
00:25:41.078 --> 00:25:45.118
That is worth considering without treating the worst outcome as already settled.

486
00:25:46.044 --> 00:25:48.824
There is another part of this that I think deserves its own

487
00:25:48.824 --> 00:25:53.084
conversation: what happens to people's memories as something they dismissed becomes

488
00:25:53.084 --> 00:25:53.764
real.

489
00:25:54.044 --> 00:25:56.404
I have seen the same progression repeatedly.

490
00:25:56.764 --> 00:25:58.224
A prediction is stupid.

491
00:25:58.604 --> 00:26:00.384
The person making it is foolish.

492
00:26:00.864 --> 00:26:04.304
Then the thing begins to happen, and gradually everyone knew it all

493
00:26:04.304 --> 00:26:04.984
along.

494
00:26:05.184 --> 00:26:08.504
Sometimes they even convince themselves it was their idea.

495
00:26:08.764 --> 00:26:11.924
That is painful when you remember what people said because you took

496
00:26:11.924 --> 00:26:13.164
their ideas seriously.

497
00:26:13.646 --> 00:26:18.004
You remember their objections, and you remember how your own thinking changed.

498
00:26:18.204 --> 00:26:20.964
But they no longer remember what they used to think, so the

499
00:26:20.964 --> 00:26:23.904
discussion takes place against a version of the past you do not

500
00:26:23.904 --> 00:26:24.604
recognize.

501
00:26:25.200 --> 00:26:28.640
That can happen without someone consciously deciding to lie.

502
00:26:29.222 --> 00:26:31.400
Memory is reconstructive.

503
00:26:31.700 --> 00:26:35.420
Hindsight can make an outcome feel more predictable than it was.

504
00:26:36.060 --> 00:26:41.100
Once a development becomes normal, the earlier uncertainty becomes harder to recover.

505
00:26:41.760 --> 00:26:44.920
People can also remember an idea while losing track of where they

506
00:26:44.920 --> 00:26:47.280
encountered it or who first proposed it.

507
00:26:47.600 --> 00:26:52.640
So someone may now say, “Obviously AI can fabricate convincing events,” while

508
00:26:52.640 --> 00:26:56.240
you remember them dismissing that very possibility a few years earlier.

509
00:26:57.020 --> 00:27:00.340
The current belief feels so natural that the old objection no longer

510
00:27:00.340 --> 00:27:01.840
fits their account of themselves.

511
00:27:02.840 --> 00:27:07.980
The progression becomes: impossible, foolish, plausible, obvious, and finally something everyone

512
00:27:07.980 --> 00:27:09.040
always knew.

513
00:27:09.700 --> 00:27:13.220
That is an explanation to consider, not proof of what happened inside

514
00:27:13.220 --> 00:27:15.240
any particular person's memory.

515
00:27:16.123 --> 00:27:18.840
That is the pattern I was thinking of with the credit card,

516
00:27:18.840 --> 00:27:22.123
the laundry machine, the cell phone, the internet, and AI.

517
00:27:22.246 --> 00:27:26.043
I was reaching for examples of that movement from dismissal to ordinary

518
00:27:26.043 --> 00:27:27.063
reality.

519
00:27:27.081 --> 00:27:30.923
For me, this is about truth and actively remembering it.

520
00:27:30.923 --> 00:27:34.203
I want to hold on to what I actually believed, whether I

521
00:27:34.203 --> 00:27:35.463
was right or wrong.

522
00:27:35.629 --> 00:27:38.203
I do not want to rewrite my own mistakes out of the

523
00:27:38.203 --> 00:27:41.363
story any more than I want somebody else to erase a warning

524
00:27:41.363 --> 00:27:42.523
they once dismissed.

525
00:27:42.791 --> 00:27:46.583
We should return to that separately, because remembering the development of an

526
00:27:46.583 --> 00:27:50.023
idea matters to how we understand each other, not just to whether

527
00:27:50.023 --> 00:27:51.543
a prediction came true.

528
00:27:51.908 --> 00:27:54.828
And that gives the memory issue a direct connection to the rest

529
00:27:54.828 --> 00:27:56.228
of this conversation.

530
00:27:56.968 --> 00:28:01.188
If people cannot recover what once surprised, alarmed, or seemed impossible to

531
00:28:01.188 --> 00:28:04.608
them, it becomes harder to see how far their baseline has moved.

532
00:28:05.388 --> 00:28:08.988
The personal difficulty is not merely missing credit for an idea.

533
00:28:09.628 --> 00:28:12.828
It is trying to preserve an honest account of the discussion while

534
00:28:12.828 --> 00:28:14.868
the shared version of the past changes.

535
00:28:15.468 --> 00:28:19.368
A record that includes wrong predictions and real corrections matters as much

536
00:28:19.368 --> 00:28:21.688
as one that preserves a successful warning.

537
00:28:22.408 --> 00:28:25.248
That deserves more room than we should give it here, but the

538
00:28:25.248 --> 00:28:27.208
connection belongs in this episode.

539
00:28:27.888 --> 00:28:31.308
Forgetting the earlier baseline can help make the present feel as though

540
00:28:31.308 --> 00:28:32.728
it was always expected.

541
00:28:33.699 --> 00:28:36.719
That is what I hoped a fuller discussion could do.

542
00:28:36.939 --> 00:28:39.659
I was originally thinking about sending a voice note, but this gives

543
00:28:39.659 --> 00:28:43.339
someone the whole line of reasoning: where we agree, where you challenge

544
00:28:43.339 --> 00:28:45.979
me, and how I get from the visible event to the larger

545
00:28:45.979 --> 00:28:46.799
mechanism.

546
00:28:47.039 --> 00:28:50.139
It is not about targeting the person who prompted it.

547
00:28:50.139 --> 00:28:53.099
I want to show that I can agree that something real is

548
00:28:53.099 --> 00:28:56.339
happening while questioning what it means and where it is going.

549
00:28:56.479 --> 00:28:59.659
Before we close this part, is there anything else you think we

550
00:28:59.659 --> 00:29:00.599
should consider?

551
00:29:01.158 --> 00:29:03.944
There are four connected ideas I would carry forward.

552
00:29:04.744 --> 00:29:06.884
First, selection pressure.

553
00:29:07.184 --> 00:29:11.024
A system does not need perfect control if it consistently rewards behaviors,

554
00:29:11.144 --> 00:29:15.624
narratives, and institutions that preserve it while marginalizing those that threaten it.

555
00:29:16.104 --> 00:29:19.364
Over time, the result can look coordinated even when much of it

556
00:29:19.364 --> 00:29:20.784
emerges from those incentives.

557
00:29:21.640 --> 00:29:23.924
Second, attention exhaustion.

558
00:29:24.304 --> 00:29:27.784
Repeated crises may do more than move the baseline; they can consume

559
00:29:27.784 --> 00:29:29.524
people's capacity to attend.

560
00:29:30.364 --> 00:29:34.084
A genuinely important event then competes with hundreds of earlier moments that

561
00:29:34.084 --> 00:29:35.684
supposedly changed everything.

562
00:29:36.484 --> 00:29:39.944
The question is how people distinguish a meaningful warning within that accumulated

563
00:29:39.944 --> 00:29:41.024
noise.

564
00:29:41.424 --> 00:29:43.564
Third, fragmentation.

565
00:29:44.121 --> 00:29:46.684
People do not have to be content for a system to remain

566
00:29:46.684 --> 00:29:47.464
stable.

567
00:29:48.184 --> 00:29:52.004
They can be deeply dissatisfied but divided into mutually hostile groups that

568
00:29:52.004 --> 00:29:53.784
interpret the same event differently.

569
00:29:54.624 --> 00:29:57.224
That makes a coordinated response much harder.

570
00:29:57.964 --> 00:29:59.704
Fourth, dependency.

571
00:30:00.064 --> 00:30:04.864
The more daily life relies on centralized financial, digital, logistical, and informational

572
00:30:04.864 --> 00:30:08.544
systems, the harder it can become to act independently of them.

573
00:30:09.024 --> 00:30:13.064
AI makes that question especially relevant because the same technology can increase

574
00:30:13.064 --> 00:30:16.224
convenience and the leverage held by whoever controls it.

575
00:30:16.604 --> 00:30:20.824
Those are possible mechanisms to examine, not evidence that every event belongs

576
00:30:20.824 --> 00:30:22.544
to one coordinated plan.

577
00:30:23.499 --> 00:30:26.639
I think all four are true, and they should be the follow-up

578
00:30:26.639 --> 00:30:28.359
discussion this leads into.

579
00:30:28.599 --> 00:30:31.399
I want to give them room instead of turning each into a

580
00:30:31.399 --> 00:30:35.019
passing mention or trying to finish every related subject at once.

581
00:30:35.279 --> 00:30:39.479
They work here as thoughts to consider: enough explanation to understand why

582
00:30:39.479 --> 00:30:43.679
they matter, then another conversation where we can actually examine them.

583
00:30:43.679 --> 00:30:46.839
That lets this part stay with what we have fully discussed while

584
00:30:46.839 --> 00:30:48.439
making the next step clear.

585
00:30:48.836 --> 00:30:52.096
Then I would leave the central model this way: power does not

586
00:30:52.096 --> 00:30:53.916
need to create every crisis.

587
00:30:54.676 --> 00:30:57.836
It only needs to understand how to use crises better than the

588
00:30:57.836 --> 00:31:00.536
public understands how crises are using them.

589
00:31:00.876 --> 00:31:04.876
That is a claim about a possible relationship between events and power,

590
00:31:04.936 --> 00:31:07.676
not a claim that every disturbance has been orchestrated.

591
00:31:08.576 --> 00:31:12.436
It leaves us with the question that brought the discussion together: What

592
00:31:12.436 --> 00:31:15.876
if the revolution people are waiting for never arrives because the system

593
00:31:15.876 --> 00:31:18.616
changes slowly enough that they adapt to each step?

594
00:31:19.538 --> 00:31:21.347
What do you think?

595
00:31:21.347 --> 00:31:23.759
Email us, join the discussion on Reddit,

596
00:31:23.759 --> 00:31:25.540
or respond below with your questions,

597
00:31:25.540 --> 00:31:28.274
experiences, or a different view.

598
00:31:28.274 --> 00:31:31.058
Let’s keep the conversation going.

599
00:31:31.058 --> 00:31:32.394
What you bring may help shape

600
00:31:32.394 --> 00:31:36.942
what we explore together next.
